files={'B':'sources/texts/api/ega_v_cote24_blass_combined.tex','Q':'sources/texts/api/tapis_de_quillen_162-5_combined.tex','C':'sources/texts/codex/K162-5-01_transcription.tex','D':'sources/texts/codex/K162-5-02_transcription.tex'}
cs=[];items={}
def c(n,title,key,scans,loc,his,statement,status,check,fate,refs,cl='B',problem='',confidence='high'):
 cid=f'C-SA23-forms-{len(cs)+1:03d}';iid=f'SA23-{800+n}';cote='24' if key=='B' else '162-5';location=files[key]+':'+loc
 if iid not in items:items[iid]={'id':iid,'sources':[],'kind':'claim and self-flagged step' if problem else 'claim','cote':cote,'scans':scans,'lines':location,'his_words':his,'statement':title,'reconstruction':'The modern statement and qualifications belong to this run; no transcription has been changed.','check':'agrees','check_reason':'Directly read supplied transcription. This owner has no indexed Pro entry; no Stage1 verdict is inferred from a heading.'}
 else:
  i=items[iid];i['lines']+='; '+location;i['scans']=sorted(set(i['scans']+scans));i['kind']='claim and self-flagged step' if problem else i['kind']
 cs.append({'id':cid,'items':[iid],'cote':cote,'scans':scans,'lines':location,'his_statement':his,'statement':statement,'status_on_pages':status,'computation':check,'class':cl,'fate':fate,'fate_date':'Manuscript dates and external publication dates are separated in references.bib; current assessment19November2026.','present_status':'As of19November2026: '+fate,'refs':refs.split(';') if refs else [],'confidence':confidence,'open_flag':problem})
# Blass: every mathematical section, together with the explicit editorial gaps.
c(1,'Hyperplanes, incidence and arbitrary base change','B',[3,4,5,6,7,8],'87-315','hyperplanes','For E finite locally free, use the quotient convention P(E). Hyperplanes are parametrized by P(E∨); the universal incidence divisor in P(E)×P(E∨) is a projective bundle over either factor and commutes with base change. A line subbundle L⁻¹→E defines P(E/L⁻¹).','Definitions and elementary derivation.','Check the universal pairing and its kernel locally on a free basis. The tautological section has bidegree(1,1); its two projective-bundle descriptions follow from kernels of locally split surjections.','Established projective-bundle construction; Stacks Constructions projective-bundle section matches quotient convention. The displayed direct-image/star and twist mistakes remain in readings.','forms_StacksProj','E')
c(1,'Divisor parameter convention','B',[5,6],'149-221','Div','Degree-d relative effective Cartier divisors in projective space, with the appropriate flatness and rank hypotheses, are parametrized by lines of degree-d equations. With P(E) quotient convention the parameter is P((Sym^dE)∨), not P(Sym^dE).','Representability assertion with a duality mismatch.','Degree1 must recover P(E∨). Over a field the polynomial equation is defined up to a nonzero scalar; over a base it is a line subbundle whose section is fiberwise regular. Rank1/empty divisor degree0 need separate treatment.','Standard construction is established; the literal printed formula has the wrong dual. The criterion is not asserted for arbitrary non-Cartier or non-flat divisors.','forms_StacksProj;forms_StacksDivisor','C',problem='Check the dual and exact divisor functor.')
c(2,'Generic hyperplanes, emptiness and irreducibility','B',[9,10,11],'317-393','Y_eta','For f:X→P over a field with X finite type, the generic hyperplane pullback of an irreducible X is empty when f(X) is a point and otherwise irreducible, with incidence dominating X. For any subset Z⊂P, its inverse image in the generic hyperplane is empty iff every point of Z is closed. For constructible Z⊂X, its generic pullback is empty iff f(Z) is finite.','Propositions2.1–2.3, proof sketched.','An affine incidence equation Σt_iS_i=1 eliminates one coefficient after inverting t_i. A positive-dimensional projective closure meets every hyperplane; a finite set is avoided by the generic hyperplane.','Classical generic-section facts; Stacks Bertini irreducibility proof uses the same incidence algebra, with nonempty fibers and base-locus hypotheses explicit.','forms_StacksBertini','E')
c(3,'Containment and component dimensions','B',[11,12],'395-487','if and only if','Positive-dimensional-image irreducible closed subsets retain containment under generic hyperplane pullback. Equality of pullbacks detects equality only when both compared subsets meet the necessary irreducibility/no invisible component hypotheses. Components with positive-dimensional image give generic components of dimension dimZ−1.','Corollaries2.4–2.6 and Proposition2.7.','If Z is a line in P² and Z′=Z∪{p}, p off Z, then their generic hyperplane pullbacks agree but Z≠Z′. This satisfies the printed irreducibility assumption on Z alone. Under irreducibility of both, the dense incidence argument proves containment.','Dimension statements are standard. The literal equality criterion omits a condition on Z′ and is refuted by the displayed example; no modern theorem with the omitted hypothesis can repair it silently.','forms_StacksBertini;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Which irreducibility hypothesis belongs in Corollary2.4?')
c(4,'Associated cycles and local module properties','B',[12,13,14,17,18],'455-560,663-700','coprof; S_k','The generic incidence map to X is regular with geometrically integral fibers. Associated cycles with nonzero-dimensional image correspond after pullback. For coherent modules, codepth bounds, Serre S_k, and Cohen–Macaulayness are preserved and reflected at corresponding points; bad-locus avoidance reduces to finite image.','Propositions2.7–2.8 and Corollaries2.9–2.11,2.16–2.17; several historical terms unclear.','Use the flat local depth formula and dimension formula for regular fibers. A regular quotient decreases both depth and support dimension by1. Historical module “separable/integral/reduced” terminology is not equated with a modern property without definition.','The depth and associated-point assertions have established flat-base-change formulations. The undefined module terminology and radicial multiplicity phrase remain qualified, not certified as an exact contemporary theorem.','forms_StacksDepth;forms_StacksAss;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Resolve the module separability and embedded-component wording.',confidence='medium')
c(5,'Smoothness Bertini and the proof shortcut','B',[15,16,17,18,19],'563-660,698-715','never vanishes','For f unramified and X finite type over k, smoothness at x is equivalent to smoothness of the generic hyperplane section at points above x. In characteristic0 unramifiedness may be dropped for the generic statement. Geometric R_k, reducedness and normality variants require their stated hypotheses.','Theorem2.12 and Corollaries2.13–2.15; proof sketched.','The standalone claim that a generic linear combination of generators of a nonzero vector bundle never vanishes is false: 1,x generate O_A1 and S1+S2x vanishes at−S1/S2. The incidence equation must also be used. Conormal incidence has dimension r−1, giving the corrected smoothness proof.','The theorem is established Bertini; Stacks33.47.3 and SGA7XVII3.1–3.3 supply exact geometric mechanisms. The shortcut is a proof gap, not a counterexample to the theorem.','forms_StacksSmoothBertini;forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Supply the missing incidence argument in Theorem2.12.')
c(6,'Bertini–Zariski and the primary-field lemma','B',[19,20,21],'717-790','I leave it up to you','For geometrically irreducible X and dim f(X)≥2, the generic hyperplane section is geometrically irreducible. In fields: for K/k finitely generated and primary, coefficients a_i generating transcendence degree≥2 give K(S_1,…,S_m) primary over k(S_1,…,S_m,a_0+Σa_iS_i).','Theorem3.1 reduced to Lemma3.1.1; proof of lemma explicitly delegated to literature.','Eliminate S_n: the extension over K has n−1 independent variables, and a_i=−t_i/t_n. The displayed n variables and positive signs are not literally correct. The modern proof uses translations of independent variables and separable algebraic closure.','Stacks37.32.1–37.32.3, following Jouanolou, proves the precise geometric irreducibility mechanism. The historical primary terminology means separable algebraic closure of k in K is k; it does not require K/k separable.','forms_StacksBertini;forms_Jouanolou','B',problem='Find and insert a proof of the explicitly outsourced Zariski lemma.')
c(7,'Geometric connectedness of the generic section','B',[21],'792-812','sigma-connected','Over algebraically closed k, assume every irreducible component has image dimension≥2 and X remains connected after removal of any closed finite-image subset. Then the generic section is geometrically connected.','Corollary3.3; chain lemma requested elsewhere.','The finite graph of irreducible components has an edge when the intersection has positive-dimensional image. The deletion hypothesis makes that graph connected; Bertini irreducibility and nonempty pullback of every edge prove geometric connectedness.','Established from the generic irreducibility theorem and the elementary component graph argument. The source prints f(X) in the definition of the exceptional family where f(Z) is necessary.','forms_StacksBertini;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='Write the component-chain lemma with the correct finite-image exceptional family.')
c(8,'Constructibility and sufficiently general sections','B',[22,23,24,25],'830-942','constructible','For a finite-presentation family, constructible fiber properties give constructible parameter loci; containing the generic point is equivalent to containing a dense open. On projective space over a field the closed-point test is valid by the Jacobson property. The listed coherent-module and geometric properties require the indicated flatness/geometric qualifications.','Propositions4.2–4.3 and introductory discussion.','A constructible subset of an irreducible noetherian space contains its generic point iff it contains a nonempty open. A constructible set containing all closed points of an open Jacobson space contains that open. Source characteristic0 AND unramified can be weakened to OR according to§2.','EGA constructibility results and Stacks fiber-property sections establish these mechanisms. The manuscript’s unavailable paragraph9 references are not treated as proof of an unidentified property list.','forms_StacksFibers;forms_StacksConstructible','A')
c(9,'Connectedness dimension and its correction','B',[25,30,31],'944-975,1175-1191','error in the statement','The claimed C_(m+1)→C_m generic-section theorem needs quasi-finite f or a replacement of dimensions by image dimensions. The source itself calls for this correction and a generalized component-chain proof.','Corollary4.4 explicitly regretted; later generalization requested.','Counterexample for arbitrary f: X=(two projective planes meeting in one point)×P², projected to their union. X stays connected after removing any dimension≤1 closed subset, but the generic hyperplane section is disconnected because it avoids the image intersection point.','The image-dimension formulation follows by the component graph and dimension-drop argument; the unrestricted written formulation is false. The line “not valid if f quasi-finite” conflicts with the proposed fix and is retained as a likely missing “unless.”','forms_StacksBertini;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Replace intrinsic by image dimension in Corollary4.4; determine the missing AQT reference.')
c(10,'Fiberwise regularity, flat quotient and open good loci','B',[26,27,28,29],'977-1135','locate reference','For F finitely presented and S-flat, the locus where a section is fiberwise F-regular is open and its quotient is flat over the parameter. Generic hyperplanes are F-regular. With proper support, fiberwise open properties of the flat quotient give open good-parameter loci; without properness/flatness only appropriate constructibility survives.','Propositions5.3–5.7; explicit request for property lists and references.','Local criterion: injectivity on each residue fiber plus flatness of F yields flatness of the cokernel. Properness makes the image of the closed bad locus closed. The proof needs inequality rather than equality in line1057 and F-flatness in5.4.','Established relative effective Cartier divisor and fiberwise-flat quotient results; Stacks Divisors and More on Morphisms give modern precise formulations. The absent lists12.1.1/12.1.4 cannot be reconstructed as a verbatim theorem.','forms_StacksDivisor;forms_StacksFibers','C',problem='Expand the missing property lists in Theorem5.6 and verify the correct flatness base.')
c(11,'All hyperplane sections connected','B',[30,31],'1144-1191','X proper over k suppose','Under§3.3 hypotheses and X proper over algebraically closed k, every hyperplane section is geometrically connected, including those of excessive dimension.','Proposition6.1; properness inserted.','All incidence components dominate the normal dual projective space. The proper Stein factor has generic degree1 and no extra vertical components; normality forces connected fibers. Properness is indispensable.','Established Zariski connectedness/Stein factorization specialization, in the exact dominant-component setting. The inserted properness is included in the modern statement.','forms_StacksStein','A',problem='Generalize the connectedness statement as requested after6.1.')
c(12,'Rational parameters and finite residue extensions','B',[31,32,33,34],'1193-1297','finite etale extension','Over an infinite field, every nonempty open of a projective space or Grassmannian has a rational point. A residue-rational parameter lifts over a Zariski neighborhood. Over a finite residue field, choose a closed point of the good open and lift its finite separable extension to a finite étale cover of a neighborhood.','Section7.1 and Propositions7.2–7.3.','Use affine charts and nonzero-polynomial avoidance over an infinite field; a monic lift of a separable residue polynomial becomes finite étale after shrinking where its discriminant is invertible. The good open condition survives that shrinking.','Classical parameter-space and étale-neighborhood constructions. Iterated regular hyperplanes preserve flatness and reduce bad-locus dimensions under the earlier hypotheses; the illegible geometric-integrality reservation is retained.','forms_StacksGrass;forms_StacksEtale;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='State the precise module/geometric reservations in the iterated construction.')
c(13,'Finite étale multisections and projectivity','B',[34,35],'1299-1338','flat proper; non-projective','For a flat projective finite-presentation family having sufficiently generically geometrically reduced fibers, successive suitable hyperplanes produce a finite étale multisection locally on the base, after a finite étale residue extension when needed. The source’s unrestricted proper version contradicts its next remark.','Proposition7.4 preceded by “Text crossed out”; Remark7.5 explicitly limits the positive statement to projectivity.','Cut down within the geometrically smooth locus to a finite reduced residue fiber, keep regularity/flatness and properness, then shrink to finite étale. The supplied cancellation marker does not specify its full extent. No general smooth proper result is inferred.','The projective construction is established. Exact unrestricted proper assertion is not retained; Remark7.5 is an authorial counterexample lead but gives no example. Search did not identify its intended specific proper nonprojective example.','forms_StacksQuasiSections;forms_StacksEtale','C',problem='Identify the intended nonprojective proper counterexample and the extent of the cancellation.',confidence='medium')
c(14,'Codimension of irregular hyperplanes','B',[36,37],'1349-1417','codimension two','If every associated cycle T of coherent F has dim f(T)>0, hyperplanes containing an associated cycle form a finite union of linear subspaces of codimension≥2. Thus F-regular sections fail only on codimension≥2; regular quotient preserves CM/codepth and changes S_k to S_(k−1).','Section8.2; strongest assertion proposed as the main statement.','A positive-dimensional image spans at least a line, and hyperplanes containing that span have codimension≥2. Multiplication by a regular equation decreases depth and support dimension by1.','Established elementary linear algebra and depth lemma. The first printed claim “codimension two” is interpreted as a bound, not exact equality; the unreadable threshold is bypassed by the explicit later positive-dimensional-image statement.','forms_StacksDepth;forms_StacksAss;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='Recover the unreadable first threshold without replacing the explicit later statement.')
c(15,'Codimension-one failure of Serre conditions','B',[38],'1426-1453','not Cohen-Macauley','A non-CM point of an integral surface gives embedded associated points in every regular hyperplane section through it; a depth2 point on a normal3-fold gives failure of S2 in such sections. These bad hyperplanes form a divisor in the dual space.','Examples and incomplete footnote8.3.','At a local ring of depth1 and dimension2, a nonzerodivisor quotient has depth0 and dimension1. At depth2/dimension3 it has depth1/dimension2. Hyperplanes through a fixed closed point have codimension1.','Established depth computation explains the examples, conditional on the asserted varieties. The five unreadable lines are not promoted to a recoverable additional theorem.','forms_StacksDepth;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='Recover the missing five-line footnote; identify an intended geometric example.',confidence='medium')
c(16,'Codimension drop and incomplete exceptional criterion','B',[39,40,41],'1462-1547','verification ... immediate','For a principal section Y and closed T with codim(T,X)≥k, intersection with Y lowers codimension by at most1 under the local dimension conventions. Equality is controlled by components T_i contained in Y and maximal-dimensional local components of X not contained in Y. The global codimension-one exceptional locus consists of point-image hyperplanes subject to these component conditions.','Lemma8.4.1 and Proposition8.4; missing clause in the stated iff criterion.','Localize at a generic point of T∩Y and apply principal-ideal dimension bounds. The source’s hypothesis codim(Y,X)≥K must be codim(T,X)≥k for the displayed conclusion; e.g. T=X disproves the literal hypothesis.','The dimension mechanism is standard. The exact global necessary-and-sufficient clause is partly unreadable and is not certified; the clear proof determines its intended component condition but is labelled reconstruction.','forms_StacksDimension;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Complete the missing component clause in8.4 and correct the local lemma hypothesis.',confidence='medium')
c(17,'Depth threshold for preserving S_k outside codimension2','B',[41,42],'1549-1602','For every closed point','For a finite map to projective space and F satisfying S_k, codimension≥2 failure of S_k in regular hyperplane sections requires depthF_x≥k+1 at closed points with dimF_x≥k+1. The draft gives a general criterion using codepth strata Z_n but leaves their definition blank.','Corollary8.5 incomplete; simplified criterion8.6 and proof.','If depthF_x=k and dimF_x≥k+1, every regular section through x has depth k−1 and dimension≥k, hence fails S_k; this gives a whole hyperplane of bad parameters. Sufficiency uses the codepth stratification and8.4, whose exact missing definition remains a limitation.','The necessary obstruction and the finite-map depth formulation are standard; the general unreadable Z_n iff statement is not fully identified. Benoist’s Theorem1.5 gives a later exact depth1 obstruction for integrality, not all S_k.','forms_StacksDepth;forms_Benoist2011;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Recover Z_n and the reversed inequalities in8.5–8.6.',confidence='medium')
c(18,'Universal singular locus and conormal geometry','B',[42,43,44,45,46,47,48],'1603-1795','P(N)','For smooth X/S and unramified f:X→P(E), the kernel of f*Ω_P/S→Ω_X/S is locally free. The Euler sequence embeds the corresponding conormal projective bundle in X×P(E∨); it represents singular zeros of the universal section. For X of relative dimension d in P of dimension r its incidence dimension is r−1.','Intrinsic diagram chase and Theorem8.7(a).','In coordinates the section and its first derivatives vanish exactly when the hyperplane covector annihilates the tangent space. This proves the functorial zero-scheme identity; quotient versus lines convention changes N/dual notation, not the geometry.','SGA7XVII3.1 and3.3 give the published conormal construction and coordinate calculation. The draft’s missing/ambiguous diagram labels and twists are listed independently.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_StacksProj','A',problem='Verify the intrinsic differential diagram and restore its labels.')
c(19,'Ordinary versus non-supersingular quadratic points','B',[48,49,63,64],'1797-1845,2366-2389','ordinary singular point','The conormal projection is unramified exactly when the hyperplane section has nondegenerate polar Hessian. This agrees with ordinary quadratic singularity if characteristic≠2 or the local quadratic rank is even. In characteristic2 odd rank, an ordinary quadratic singularity may have degenerate polar Hessian.','Theorem8.7(b) conflates the notions; propagated into8.8 and9.2.','Over F2 the smooth conic XZ+Y² has Gauss map[Z:0:X]; on its parametrization this is Frobenius and is everywhere ramified, although tangent sections are ordinary double points. The Hessian of x² is zero.','Precisely corrected in SGA7XVII1.1–1.2 and Proposition3.3; Example3.4 gives the inseparable Frobenius Gauss map. Parent’s cote25 scans140–141 also records the correction; both endpoints are linked only after direct read.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Correct the ordinary/non-supersingular equivalence in characteristic2.')
c(20,'Reflexive duality and the smooth dual locus','B',[48,49,50,51,52],'1806-1956','generically etale ... birational','For a smooth integral projective embedding, generically unramified conormal projection is birational onto the dual hypersurface and is an isomorphism over its smooth locus. Bitangent hyperplanes can give unramified points over singular dual points without étaleness. For merely unramified f, birationality and unique tangent point need not hold.','Theorem8.8 and Corollary8.9, partly missing heading; Remark8.10;9.2 acknowledges needed nonimmersion extension.','The tangent hyperplane to the dual recovers f(x), hence x only for an embedding. Over C take a plane elliptic curve and compose its embedding with a nontrivial étale isogeny; the conormal map has separable degree>1. This refutes the unrestricted version while preserving the embedded one.','SGA7XVII Proposition3.5 gives the exact embedded theorem and identifies the three open loci. The source itself warns in9.2 that8.8 must be reformulated for nonimmersions.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Separate the immersion theorem from its requested unramified extension.')
c(21,'Dual defect, inseparable examples and terminology','B',[52,53,58],'1958-2016,2184-2191','too lazy to construct the examples','In characteristic0 the image of the supersingular incidence has codimension≥2; generically finite conormal maps are separable. In characteristic p, smooth hypersurfaces can have finite purely inseparable Gauss map. “Ruled” here means dual-defective in the intended embedding and is not the standard ruled-variety condition.','Corollary8.11; explicit example request8.12; terminology withdrawn8.17.','For ΣX_i^(p+1)=0, partial derivatives are X_i^p and the Gauss map is Frobenius. The hypersurface is smooth because all derivatives cannot vanish at a projective point. A smooth quadric surface is ruled in the classical sense but not dual-defective.','SGA7XVII Example3.4 explicitly supplies this family;3.5.0 gives the characteristic/parity boundary. Thus the requested example is established, and the draft’s terminology is retracted rather than adopted.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','A',problem='Supply the positive-characteristic example and replace the retracted word “ruled.”')
c(22,'Exceptional regularity and normality criteria','B',[54,55,56],'2033-2140','I may have somewhat exaggerated','The draft combines low-dimensional dual-defective components and closed-point depth thresholds to force codimension≥2 bad loci for geometric R_k, reducedness and normality. It later weakens unramifiedness to a large smooth open and doubts a blanket converse for R_k.','Proposition8.13, Corollary8.14, Remark8.15, with illegible indices and an explicit reservation.','The clear reduced/normal components use R0+S1 and R1+S2. A depth defect at a closed point forces a divisorial family of bad hyperplanes by the regular quotient calculation. The unreadable low-dimensional R_k bound and withdrawn converse are not asserted exactly.','Classical Serre criteria and SGA conormal estimates explain the recoverable parts. No exact theorem matching the damaged global iff statement was located; this is a textual/hypothesis limit, not evidence of an open modern conjecture.','forms_StacksDepth;forms_SGA7Katz;forms_Benoist2011','C',problem='Determine the missing R_k indices and the additional conditions admitted in8.15.',confidence='medium')
c(23,'Normal integral sections and exceptional-locus bounds','B',[56,57],'2126-2173','prof_x X at least three','For geometrically integral proper X, geometrically normal, depth≥3 at closed points, and f unramified off codimension≥2, the draft asserts a codimension≥2 complement to normal integral hyperplane sections. A smooth quadric surface shows the dimension/depth restriction matters.','Proposition8.16 and Remarks8.17.','Normality plus connectedness gives integrality. A tangent plane to X0X3−X1X2=0 cuts two intersecting lines, so the bad family is the dual quadric divisor. Finite/nonempty equidimensional-fiber hypotheses must be checked when comparing modern stronger bounds.','Benoist2011 Theorems1.4–1.5 and Poonen–Slavov Theorem1.5/1.8 give exact later bounds with distinct immersion/equidimensional-fiber hypotheses. They support the embedded case and do not silently establish every arbitrary-morphism clause.','forms_Benoist2011;forms_PoonenSlavov;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Match8.16 to a precise modern exceptional-locus theorem under its weaker morphism hypotheses.')
c(24,'Special linear systems and fixed components','B',[58,59,60,61,62],'2193-2336','generic element ... irreducible','For a linear subsystem with base locus B, geometric irreducibility of the generic moving section follows from image dimension≥2 under projection, provided B contributes no divisorial component. In image dimension1 the image must be a line with geometrically irreducible generic fiber. The draft uses this for sections through a singular point or tangent space.','Proposition8.18 and subsequent reduction; several words and dimension labels damaged.','For the blow-up of P² at p, pullbacks of all lines through p have fixed exceptional divisor; removing B gives an irreducible moving part but the whole divisor is reducible. Thus density of X\\B in every generic section requires a base-locus codimension condition.','Stacks37.32.3 explicitly includes codim base locus≥2. Poonen–Slavov Example1.7 records the analogous blow-up obstruction. The unreadable singular-versus-nonsingular condition8.18(b) is not decided from context alone.','forms_StacksBertini;forms_PoonenSlavov;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Recover8.18(b), fix dim1 to d−1 where justified, and state the base-locus condition.')
c(25,'Veronese embeddings and Lefschetz pencils','B',[63,64,66,69,70,71],'2345-2389,2467-2475,2586-2645','n at least two','For smooth projective embedded X over an algebraically closed field, every d-th Veronese embedding with d≥2 admits Lefschetz pencils: smooth transverse axis, generically smooth fibers, and at most one ordinary quadratic singularity in each singular fiber. In characteristic0 the original embedding suffices. The non-supersingular strengthening needs the parity qualification.','Proposition9.2, missing Corollary9.4/Theorem9.5, and pencil applications.','Degree≥2 sections realize arbitrary quadratic jets locally. This creates ordinary quadratic singularities, but cannot make an odd-rank characteristic2 Hessian invertible. A singular point is rational over the residue field of its parameter, not necessarily over the ground field.','Exact published statement: SGA7XVII Theorem2.5.1–2.5.2 with Definition2.2; it proves existence of ordinary Lefschetz pencils and does not equate them with nondegenerate Hessians in every characteristic.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','A',problem='Write the missing recapitulating theorem without importing the false Hessian strengthening.')
c(26,'Prescribed-point and tangent irreducibility after Veronese','B',[64,65,66],'2395-2465','proof is essentially trivial','For a geometrically irreducible X with image dimension≥2 and no divisorial fixed base components, generic degree≥2 sections through a point or with the prescribed linear tangency remain geometrically irreducible. For the full tangent-space condition use degree≥3 in the exceptional plane case.','Proposition9.3, explicit polynomial construction.','The ratios of T1,T1²,T1T2 recover two algebraically independent functions; in the cubic plane case use T_r²,T_r³,T_r²T_(r−1), with Q/P=T_r (not Q/R). For dimL≥3, ratios of three independent squares remain algebraically independent even in characteristic2.','The construction verifies the image-dimension criterion in Stacks37.32.3. The no-fixed-divisor condition is retained from unramified/immersion use; the displayed statement alone would fail for a blow-down map.','forms_StacksBertini;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Make the retained unramified/base-locus hypothesis and polynomial ratios explicit.')
c(27,'Missing end-of-section statements','B',[66],'2467-2475','Corollary9.4; Theorem9.5','The transcription contains only the title of Corollary9.4 and an illegible Theorem9.5 with part of its hypotheses. A likely synthesis with9.2 is editorial context, not the missing statement itself.','Blank or illegible statements.','No formula is supplied from which a unique theorem can be reconstructed; the later pencil application is recorded separately.','Not identifiable exactly from the supplied transcription. SGA7XVII2.5 is a verified related published theorem, not a restoration of the absent wording.','forms_SGA7Katz','F',problem='Recover the text of9.4 and9.5 from an authorized future source.',confidence='low')
c(28,'Avoiding the exceptional locus with a line','B',[66,67],'2477-2522','one single L','For a closed subset Z⊂P^r, codimZ≥2 iff a geometric projective line avoids Z; over an infinite field such a line can be chosen rational. A single line avoiding a merely constructible Z is insufficient unless it also avoids its closure.','Section10.1; source overbar uncertain and a translator correction.','Take Z=D\\{p} where D is a line in P², and L a different line through p. Then L∩Z=∅ although closureZ has codimension1. For a closed hypersurface, restriction of its positive-degree homogeneous equation to any line has a geometric zero.','Elementary projective dimension theory proves the closed-set criterion. The constructible formulation requires the closure explicitly; the draft’s ambiguous repeated Z is not silently supplied with a bar.','forms_StacksDimension;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Determine whether the missing overbar is present in10.1.')
c(29,'Pencil incidence as a blow-up','B',[68,69,70],'2533-2584,2618-2623','isomorphic in a unique fashion','For a pencil with base ideal I=(a,b) generated locally by a regular sequence, its incidence scheme over X is Bl_I X. Regularity is intrinsic via the conormal isomorphism and regular immersion; a sufficiently general axis has this property under the usual finite-type hypotheses.','Theorem10.2; Proposition10.3 cancelled and absent.','The syzygy of a,b is generated by(b,−a), and the Rees algebra equals Sym(I)=R[U,V]/(bU−aV). Taking Proj gives the incidence equation. Uniqueness follows from the schematically dense complement of the regular center.','Established complete-intersection blow-up computation, also used in SGA7’s pencil geometry. The crossed-out Proposition10.3 has no surviving statement to classify more precisely.','forms_StacksBlowup;forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','A',problem='Supply the general regular-section lemma requested before10.2; retain10.3 as cancelled.')
c(30,'Curve pencils and quadratic ramification','B',[70,71],'2625-2645','quadratic','For a smooth geometrically connected projective embedded curve over an infinite field and sufficiently suitable degree≥2 pencil, the induced map to P¹ is generically étale and every geometric fiber has at most one double point, all other points simple. Characteristic2 may give wild quadratic ramification.','Corollary10.4.','A double local fiber has algebra k[t]/(t²). This is an ordinary zero-dimensional quadratic singularity even though its Hessian vanishes in characteristic2; thus it is compatible with SGA7’s ordinary Lefschetz statement.','SGA7XVII2.5 supplies the geometric existence theorem. Rational-parameter descent uses the open good-pencil locus over the infinite ground field; no assertion of tame ramification or all branch points rational over k is made.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_LocalChecks','A')
c(31,'Grassmannian representability and finiteness','B',[71,72],'2647-2689','quasi-compact','The rank-n locally free quotient functor of a quasi-coherent module E is represented by the Grassmannian, with Plücker map to P(Λ^nE). Finite type/finiteness of presentation of E give the corresponding projectivity/presentation statements. Arbitrary E does not give a quasi-compact Grassmannian.','Section11; exterior-power symbols and determinant omitted in transcription.','For E a countably infinite-dimensional vector space and n=1, P(E)=Proj k[x1,x2,…] has the cover D_+(xi) with no finite subcover. The coordinate point using any omitted variable witnesses failure. The Plücker line is Λ^nG, not G itself.','Standard Grassmannian construction is established; the unrestricted quasi-compactness claim is false. The finite free case is proved explicitly by Stacks Constructions; arbitrary-module representability is handled by quotient/Plücker equations with the stated qualifications.','forms_StacksGrass;forms_StacksProj;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Restore the Plücker exterior powers and remove unsupported quasi-compactness.')
c(32,'Grassmannian charts, duality and linear subspaces','B',[72,73,74],'2692-2784','Grass(E)','For E locally free of rank r and0≤n≤r, Grass_n(E) is smooth with geometrically connected fibers, covered by affine n(r−n)-charts. Residue-rational points lift locally, infinite-field opens have rational points, and duality identifies Grass_n(E) with Grass_(r−n)(E∨). Its quotient determines the corresponding linear projective subspace; variable rank is represented by the disjoint union.','Section11 chart proof and elementary projective geometry.','Choose a rank-n direct summand whose image is an isomorphism to the quotient; the remaining map is an arbitrary matrix. The dual quotient E∨/G∨ gives polarity and reverses inclusion. Empty n>r must be excluded from geometric connectedness.','Established standard Grassmannian functor and affine-chart proof. The future degree-one intrinsic characterization is only a programme here; flatness and Hilbert polynomial are needed for a full statement.','forms_StacksGrass;forms_StacksProj;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='State the later intrinsic degree-one characterization with its flatness hypotheses.')

# Tapis de Quillen. The two typed copies are consolidated, retaining the differing margins.
c(40,'Nerve and local-coefficient cohomology','Q',[2,3,32,33],'46-115,747-806','foncteur pleinement fidèle','The nerve embeds small categories fully faithfully into simplicial sets. A local system is a functor taking every arrow to an isomorphism. Its category cohomology, computed by derived inverse limit with the chosen variance, agrees with cohomology of the nerve with that local system.','Assertions credited to Quillen and Artin–Mazur; proof not given.','An n-simplex is a composable n-tuple; a simplicial map is forced by objects and arrows and respects composition through2-simplices. The standard cosimplicial replacement gives the product cochain complex. Requiring only existing isomorphisms to be inverted is tautological and is not the local-system condition.','Established classifying-space and category-cohomology theory. Thomason1980 recalls the homotopy comparison and Quillen Higher K-theory I gives the nerve formalism. The variance and local-system correction remain explicit.','forms_Thomason;forms_QuillenK;forms_LocalChecks','B')
c(41,'Twisted Whitehead tests and the category of simplices','Q',[2,3,32,33],'60-115,761-806','équivalence faible','For maps of CW realizations, the component, fundamental-groupoid and all local-coefficient homology/cohomology tests give the Whitehead criterion. The category of simplices Δ/X has nerve weakly equivalent to X, so localization of Cat at nerve weak equivalences models homotopy types. A category and its opposite have canonically homeomorphic realizations after simplex reversal.','Criterion and constructions stated; their naturality is left implicit.','The last-vertex map from the subdivided category-of-simplices construction gives the weak equivalence. With π1 fixed, apply the simply connected homological Whitehead theorem to universal covers. Opposite chains reverse order and barycentric coordinates; local systems use inverse transports.','Established. Thomason1980 pp305–306 explicitly records the localization equivalence and distinguishes it from his later model structure. No claim is made that ordinary categorification of an arbitrary simplicial set is already a homotopy inverse.','forms_Thomason;forms_Whitehead;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Make the naturality and coefficient hypotheses in the Artin–Mazur criterion explicit.')
c(42,'Products in category cochains and nonlocal coefficients','Q',[4,34],'127-136,815-824','Π; Si !','Category cochains use products over composable strings for arbitrary small categories. Nonlocal functors still have category cohomology/derived-limit interpretations, although they are not topological local systems.','The direct-sum notation is corrected in a margin; a denial of a nonlocal interpretation is answered “Si!” in the first witness.','For an infinite discrete category, H0 is the product of values. A direct sum omits sections of infinite support, already in degree0. The bar resolution computes derived limits without invertibility of transitions.','Classical derived-limit construction, as used by Quillen. The source’s own two corrections are retained rather than attributed to this run.','forms_QuillenK;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='Reconcile the two margins and keep the nonlocal interpretation distinct from local systems.')
c(43,'Locally constant derived objects and a boundedness correction','Q',[4,5,6,34,35,36],'138-217,826-896','D_lc; D^-','For a fixed nonzero coefficient ring, a weak equivalence of nerves induces the expected equivalence on locally constant derived objects in the homotopy-theoretic setting. Ext sheaves between locally constant modules are locally constant only under the stated local cohomological triviality/constant-functor hypotheses. Derived tensor products of bounded complexes need not remain bounded over an arbitrary ring.','Several equivalences asserted, with a proposed topos hypothesis; D^b is explicitly replaced by D^- for tensor products.','Over R=k[ε]/ε², the periodic resolution of k has all positive Tor_i^R(k,k)=k, so k⊗^L_Rk is unbounded below in cohomological indexing. The local-Ext assertion is checked on a trivializing neighborhood using its assumed constant-module Ext comparison; it is not transferred to arbitrary topoi.','Established for locally constant derived sheaves on spaces/appropriate locally contractible topoi. The general topos equivalence exactly as written is conditional on the strong hypothesis and not identified with an unrestricted étale statement. Modern derived-category references confirm the boundedness obstruction.','forms_Derived;forms_Whitehead;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Specify a precise topos hypothesis giving the local Ext and derived-local-system equivalences.')
c(44,'Abelian cohomology alone does not detect the homotopy type','Q',[5,6,35,36],'175-217,866-896','coefficients abéliens','The proposed simplification to cohomology with abelian coefficients alone omits the nonabelian fundamental-group test. A map to a point can be an isomorphism on every constant abelian-coefficient cohomology group and fail to be a weak equivalence.','Explicit question.','Use Hatcher Example2.38: attach two2-cells to S1∨S1 along a^5b^(−3) and b^3(ab)^(−2). The cellular boundary matrix[[5,−2],[−3,1]] has determinant−1, so the complex is acyclic. Its fundamental group surjects onto A5, verified by explicit permutations of orders5,3,2. Its map to a point passes all coefficients pulled back from the point. This does not refute a stronger test also quantifying local systems not in the essential image.','Negative under the natural pullback-coefficient reading. The usual twisted Whitehead theorem needs the fundamental-groupoid information; the exact source formulation must specify how coefficients on both sides are compared.','forms_Whitehead;forms_Hatcher;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Does the abelian-coefficient simplification include an equivalence of local-system categories?')
c(45,'Internal categories, multiple nerves and globularity','Q',[6,7,36,37],'219-285,898-966','m-Catégorie dans (n-Cat)','Internal categories in Cat are double categories, encoded by bisimplicial sets satisfying Segal conditions in each direction. Iteration gives n-fold categories and an action of the symmetric group permuting directions. Strict globular2-categories form the subcategory with a discrete object category in the specified direction.','Constructions explained; relation between m-categories in n-Cat and(m+n)-simplicial objects explicitly requested.','For a category C, the twofold nerve has entries Fun([m]×[n],C); transposition interchanges directions. Iterated internal-category structures give iterated Segal conditions. Globularity adds discreteness constraints and is not implied by those Segal conditions alone.','Established strict multiple-category/nerve theory. Modern terminology “bicategory” must not replace this manuscript’s double-category usage. The precise higher globularity convention must be stated before claiming an equivalence.','forms_MultiNerve;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Specify the higher globularity conditions in the proposed m+n comparison.')
c(46,'Internal abelian group categories and two-term complexes','Q',[8,38],'294-325,975-1009','complexes de longueur1','A category internal to abelian groups is equivalent to a chain complex C1→C0 concentrated in degrees1,0: objects C0 and arrows(x,z):x→x+dz. Every such internal category is automatically a groupoid.','Construction stated via Dold’s correspondence.','For an internal category, split source by the identity map and take C1=ker(source), with d=target on that kernel. Additivity forces composition(x,z),(x+dz,w)↦(x,z+w), and inverse(x,z)↦(x+dz,−z). These formulas give inverse functors.','Classical Dold–Kan/internal-category correspondence. Hopkins–Singer AppendixB.2 gives the corresponding strict Picard-groupoid construction.','forms_HopkinsSinger;forms_DoldKan;forms_LocalChecks','E')
c(47,'Picard strictification and the self-commutor','Q',[8,38],'302-325,985-1009','resp. commutative','A symmetric Picard groupoid need not be equivalent to one coming from a strictly commutative two-term complex. The self-commutor ε:π0/2→π1 is an obstruction. Strict Picard groupoids with ε=0 admit the complex description; general ones correspond to connective spectra with homotopy only in degrees0,1.','The commutative strengthening is deleted; objections associated with Verdier are recorded.','The groupoid of superlines over a field of characteristic≠2 has odd-line self-symmetry−1, so ε is nonzero. In the complex construction the commutor of an object with itself is the identity. Equivalence preserves ε.','Exact modern classification: Hopkins–Singer2005 AppendixB.2, DefinitionsB.5–B.6 and resultB.12. Its arXiv version first appeared13Nov2002; it is a convenient checked statement, not asserted to be the historical first proof.','forms_HopkinsSinger;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Distinguish strict commutativity from coherent symmetry in the deleted Picard assertion.')
c(48,'Higher abelian strict categories and derived Hom','Q',[9,10,39,40],'336-397,1019-1075','RHom; longueur n','Globular strict n-categories internal to abelian groups correspond to length-n chain complexes. Without globularity, n-fold internal categories instead give multicomplexes. After localization at the corresponding homology equivalences, mapping higher groupoids are governed by the appropriate truncation of derived Hom, requiring resolutions rather than the naive Hom complex.','n=2 bicomplex reduction explained; general n and the RHom formulation proposed.','In n=2 the discrete first row removes the off-axis terms, leaving a three-term chain complex; the double-complex identity becomes d²=0. Induction repeats this normalized-chain argument. For Z/2 in degree0, derived Hom has a nonzero Ext1 absent from ordinary Hom; this distinguishes the proposed derived mapping object.','The strict algebraic statement is established by Dold–Kan and internal abelian-category theory. The source does not fix the weak mapping-morphism convention, so its entire higher RHom equivalence is a qualified reconstruction, not a verbatim theorem recovered from it.','forms_DoldKan;forms_HopkinsSinger;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Choose the weak morphisms and truncation that make the RHom comparison precise.')
c(49,'Loop spaces require higher associativity data','Q',[9,39],'350-360,1033-1044','espaces des lacets ?','Looping relates connected pointed spaces to grouplike A∞ spaces with coherent multiplication; a group object in the ordinary homotopy category records only the lowest associativity/inverse data and does not supply a complete delooping structure.','Question followed by a doubtful negative marginal response.','An A∞ operad specifies an entire compatible hierarchy of homotopies. May’s recognition theorem assumes this hierarchy and connectedness in its stated form; dropping it is a different claim. The manuscript’s suggested full-faithfulness also needs the class of structured maps specified.','May1972 Theorem13.4 gives the checked positive recognition theorem. Classical non-loop homotopy-associative H-space examples show the missing coherence issue; the precise manuscript categorical equivalence is not accepted merely from a bare H-space structure.','forms_May;forms_Stasheff','C',problem='Replace the group-object category by a precise grouplike A∞ category and compare the forgetful functor.')
c(50,'Group completion and higher algebraic K-theory','Q',[10,11,40,41],'399-442,1077-1117','K_i; vraie catégories triangulées','Symmetric monoidal categories admit homotopical group-completion constructions yielding connective K-theory spectra. Exact categories require exact-sequence relations, not only direct sums. A bare triangulated homotopy category is in general insufficient to determine higher K-theory; an exact, dg, stable∞ or comparable enhancement retains the required data.','Research programme; additive versus abelian K0 is already distinguished.','For a nonsplit exact sequence, its K0 relation need not hold in split K0. The nerve of an additive category with its zero object is contractible, so the ordinary nerve cannot be its desired K-space. A monoidal groupoid’s nerve has the underlying π0,π1; its monoidal structure supplies the coherent group-like multiplication.','Developed by Quillen’s Q-construction(1973), Segal’s Γ-space approach(1974), and later Waldhausen constructions. Schlichting’s counterexample addresses the insufficiency of an unenhanced triangulated category. The manuscript does not already define a universal n-category with all these properties.','forms_QuillenK;forms_Segal;forms_Schlichting;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Compare the proposed universal categorical group completion with exact-category K-theory.')
c(51,'Unoriented bordism operations','Q',[12,13,43,44],'453-552,1137-1207','contrevariant; covariant propre','Unoriented bordism cohomology of smooth manifolds has pullbacks, external products and proper pushforwards with the degree shift determined by dimensions. Transverse Cartesian squares satisfy base change, and products satisfy the projection formula. Compactness/properness and transversality are part of the geometric statements.','Definitions and relations presented by bordism classes.','Pull back transverse representatives, and compare fiber products on the nose. Composition of proper representative maps stays proper. The bordism boundary relation makes these operations independent of choices. Nontransverse pullback requires the cohomological construction or perturbation hypotheses, not an arbitrary set-theoretic fiber product.','Classical unoriented bordism operations and Pontryagin–Thom theory; Thom1954 is the foundational checked source. The two witnesses differ in the wording of compatibility, and that difference is retained in readings.','forms_Thom;forms_LocalChecks','E')
c(52,'Universality of the geometric bordism functor','Q',[13,14,44,45],'511-590,1178-1254','universalité','The bordism functor is universal for the specified homotopy-invariant contravariant theory with proper covariant maps, transversality/base-change relations and the prescribed unit/product normalization. A closed representative f:M→X must map to f_*(1).','Proof sketched by representing generators and turning a bordism into a homotopy relation.','The only possible transformation sends[f] to f_*(1). For a bordism, doubling and the parameter map to a circle produce the boundary relation using the explicitly assumed homotopy and pushforward compatibility. The conclusion is restricted to those axioms; it is not a universal characterization among arbitrary cohomology theories.','Geometric Pontryagin–Thom constructions support the bordism description. This particular minimal-axiom universal property is checked directly in the manuscript’s framework; no separate historical first publication is inferred.','forms_Thom;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Specify exactly which product/Gysin compatibility is an axiom in the two universal-property witnesses.')
c(53,'Unoriented bordism coefficients and additive splitting','Q',[14,15,45,46],'573-653,1217-1290','polynômes; section additive','The coefficient ring MO_* is F2[x_i:i>0,i≠2^r−1]. The Thom spectrum splits additively as a wedge of shifted HF2 spectra, yielding a noncanonical natural additive splitting of its augmentation to mod2 cohomology and finite freeness of MO^*(X) over MO^* for finite CW complexes.','Thom’s theorem invoked; a marginal copy reverses the excluded generator degrees.','The coefficient of degree2 is nonzero, detected on RP² by w1²; degrees1,3,7 are excluded polynomial generators. Additive splitting and the finite number of cells give the module decomposition. It is not a canonical multiplicative or pushforward-compatible splitting.','Thom1954 TheoremsII.10 andIV.12 establish the additive and coefficient results. The precise ring degree correction is forced by IV.12 and does not decide the ink.','forms_Thom;forms_LocalChecks','E')
c(54,'Multiplicative splitting and the stronger E∞ question','Q',[14,45],'589-605,1232-1245','section multiplicative ?','There is a multiplicative splitting at the level of homotopy-commutative ring spectra/cohomology functors for MO, but this does not give MO the corresponding structure as a commutative HF2-algebra.','Question in the second witness; not asserted settled on the manuscript.','Boardman’s theorem identifies an additively graded Eilenberg–Mac Lane homotopy-commutative ring spectrum of prime characteristic multiplicatively with its graded Eilenberg–Mac Lane model. The degree0 inclusion supplies the homotopy-ring section. Szymik’s stronger commutative S-algebra obstruction concerns E∞ structures and does not negate that weaker section.','Positive weak answer: Boardman1980 Theorem1.1, stated precisely in Szymik2014 Theorem1.3. Negative stronger answer: Szymik2014 Proposition5.1 and Theorem5.6. The latter preprint appeared14Nov2012, journal2014; no1968 settlement is inferred.','forms_Boardman;forms_Szymik','B',problem='Which level of multiplicativity is intended by the manuscript question?')
c(55,'No unital section compatible with every Gysin map','Q',[14,45],'589-605,1237-1245','Quillen ignore','A unital natural section H^*(−;F2)→MO^*(−) cannot commute with all proper Gysin maps.','Compatibility with covariant maps explicitly left unknown.','For p:RP²→point, a unital section sends1 to1. Then p_*^MO(1)=[RP²]≠0, since its Stiefel–Whitney number w1² is1. But p_*^H(1) lies in H^(−2)(point;F2)=0. Naturality for this proper map would equate a nonzero class with0.','Negative; elementary counterexample using Thom1954 TheoremsIV.10–IV.12. This is a present reconstruction, not an asserted missing answer of Quillen in1968.','forms_Thom;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Can any restricted class of Gysin maps admit the desired compatibility?')
c(56,'Bordism correspondences and modules','Q',[15,16,46,47],'619-667,1262-1302','Hom(B(X),B(Y))','For closed smooth manifolds, bordism Poincaré duality and the finite-free Künneth isomorphism identify bordism correspondences with graded MO^*-linear maps between their bordism cohomology modules, with the specified dimension shifts. Adding summands and inverting the Tate shift gives the corresponding category of finite graded projective modules.','Full-faithfulness and completion asserted.','Use MO^*(X×Y)≅MO^*(X)⊗_ΛMO^*(Y) and the perfect integration pairing on a closed manifold to identify the tensor with Hom_Λ. The composition formula becomes contraction by that pairing. The argument requires closed/finite spaces. Projective modules are obtained by splitting idempotents, not by simply forgetting geometric restrictions.','Established under the finite/closed hypotheses from Thom splitting and generalized Poincaré duality. The claim that every desired representation is geometrically realized is separate and is not proved by this module equivalence.','forms_Thom;forms_BordismDuality;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Make the dimension shifts, compactness and idempotent completion explicit.')
c(57,'Coherence of the coefficient ring','Q',[15,46],'642-653,1280-1290','modules de présentation finie','The polynomial ring Λ over F2 on the unoriented bordism generators is coherent, although it is not noetherian. Consequently its finitely presented module category is abelian.','Assertion attached to the enlarged correspondence category.','Every finite presentation matrix and every finite relation among its entries descend to a polynomial ring in finitely many variables. That noetherian ring has finitely generated kernels, and extension to Λ is flat. Thus kernels of maps of finite free modules over Λ are finitely presented; coherence follows.','Classical coherent-ring argument, independently proved here. No noetherian claim is made for infinitely many generators.','forms_StacksCoherent;forms_LocalChecks','E')
c(58,'Embedded submanifolds are not annihilated by all Steenrod operations','Q',[16,17,47,48],'669-715,1304-1346','Pourquoi nécessaire ?','If S(X) literally means mod2 Poincaré duals of arbitrary embedded submanifolds, membership is not equivalent to annihilation by every positive Steenrod operation. The Thom identity includes the Stiefel–Whitney classes of the normal bundle.','Necessity is explicitly questioned; later category assertions depend on it.','The embedded RP¹⊂RP² is dual to a∈H1(RP²;F2), while Sq1(a)=a²≠0. Its normal line is nontrivial. A framed-normal condition would remove this particular obstruction, but the converse framed representability question is not established by merely annihilating operations.','The literal criterion is false. Thom’s characteristic-class formula explains the missing normal-bundle information. No alteration to “framed submanifolds” is made without ink evidence.','forms_Thom;forms_Milnor;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Determine the intended S(X) and whether a normal-framing hypothesis was suppressed.')
c(59,'Steenrod dual and strict additive formal-series automorphisms','Q',[17,48],'697-724,1332-1355','Aut(G_a)','At p=2 the dual Steenrod Hopf algebra is F2[ξ1,ξ2,…], with |ξ_i|=2^i−1 and coproduct obtained by composing strict additive series x+Σa_i x^(2^i). It represents the corresponding graded strict-automorphism group functor.','Identification proposed; group-scheme interpretation and canonical coactions discussed.','Composition gives the coefficient of x^(2^n) as Σ_i a_i b_(n−i)^(2^i), with order depending on the coaction convention. Positive grading gives the connected filtered/pro-unipotent description; the infinite polynomial coordinate ring is not a finite-type algebraic group.','Milnor1958 Appendix1, with its polynomial generators and action on the degree1 class, gives the exact algebraic result. The formal-series interpretation follows by the displayed composition identity.','forms_Milnor;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Fix left/right coaction and grading conventions in the automorphism identification.')
c(60,'Realization of all Steenrod representations','Q',[17,48],'712-734,1343-1363','toute représentation','The suggestion that a completed category of manifolds and S-correspondences realizes every finite graded representation of the dual Steenrod group requires a precise corrected definition of S and a geometric realization theorem. A bare H-space mapping to BO does not by itself specify a coherently multiplicative Thom theory.','Open programme, with no realization proof.','The counterexample to the written S-criterion blocks the proposed argument before essential surjectivity. Steenrod modules also carry instability/geometric constraints before stabilization; abstract comodules cannot be equated with manifold cohomology without the stated completion. For a multiplicative Thom spectrum, the orientation map must carry coherent multiplicative structure.','Parts are developed in stable homotopy and Thom-spectrum theory, but the exact categorical realization claim was not located in the checked sources. It remains a qualified programme, not a certified open theorem in2026.','forms_Milnor;forms_May;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Define the completed S-correspondence category and isolate a testable essential-surjectivity statement.')

# Quillen's attached publications are author-attributed, not called unpublished Grothendieck theorems.
c(61,'Positive Hermitian forms and high prolongations','Q',[50,51,52,53,54],'1373-1608','positive on pure powers','Quillen’s theorem: a Hermitian form on Sym^m(T)⊗V, for finite-dimensional complex T,V, strictly positive on every ζ^m⊗v with ζ,v nonzero becomes positive definite after multiplying by a sufficiently high power of the standard squared norm, equivalently after sufficiently high prolongation. Strict positivity and finite dimension are essential.','Complete attached1968 proof with three lemmas.','The Gaussian inner product makes monomials orthogonal with squared norm α!. Integration by parts gives adjointness of multiplication and differentiation. The normal-order identity converts the prolongation into a positive leading term and lower-degree errors; the leading binomial grows one degree faster than the error bound. Exact identities and the small scalar example are recomputed in compute_checks.py.','Published by Daniel Quillen in Inventiones5(1968)237–242, received27Feb1968. The attached offprint is that paper, not a later fate of a Grothendieck assertion. Its printed proof supplies the searched exact match.','forms_QuillenPositive;forms_LocalChecks','B')
c(62,'Norm identities and the positivity example','Q',[51,52,53,54],'1430-1608','binomial; P(N)','The Gaussian derivative norm on homogeneous degree-k polynomials satisfies the falling-factorial/binomial identity after the specified normalization; commuting polynomial differential operators past multiplication gives the finite Weyl expansion. The example(x+y)^N(x²−xy+y²) has all coefficients strictly positive starting at N=3.','Lemmas1–3 and operator calculation; a lower-order polynomial notation is ambiguous.','Compute Gaussian monomial integrals as factorials, sum multiindex derivatives by Vandermonde, and verify the one-variable Weyl identity D^a x^b=Σ_j binom(a,j)(b)_j x^(b−j)D^(a−j). Coefficients for N=1,2 have zeros; N=3 are1,2,1,1,2,1. The use of i+N versus m+N can be justified by increasing the error constant uniformly in N; it does not affect the required degree bound.','Exact calculations agree with the main proof once the normalization is kept. The ambiguous P(N) expression is recorded for reading verification; it is not silently normalized away.','forms_QuillenPositive;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Check the lower-order polynomial/binomial normalization in the printed formula.')
c(63,'Hermitian sum-of-squares consequences','Q',[54,55],'1609-1650','Corollary1; Nullstellensatz','Strictly positive bihomogeneous Hermitian forms admit the quotient-of-squared-norm representation after multiplication by a power of the norm. The corresponding elliptic differential quadratic form has a high prolongation that is a sum of squared polynomial differential operators. A finite set of homogeneous complex polynomials with no common nonzero zero generates every sufficiently high homogeneous degree; homogenization and the Rabinowitsch trick yield the affine Nullstellensatz.','Three corollaries proved in the attached paper.','The prolonged positive Hermitian matrix has a square-root/Gram factorization, giving a basis of squared holomorphic forms and the corresponding differential-operator identity. For the Nullstellensatz take E=Σ|Q_j|²; its prolonged derivative forms span the full homogeneous dual because their sum of squares is positive definite. The affine trick adds an auxiliary variable enforcing nonvanishing. No difference-of-squares corollary is attributed to this paper.','These are the actual corollaries in Quillen1968pp241–242. They are not the unrestricted real polynomial sum-of-squares theorem without denominators.','forms_QuillenPositive;forms_LocalChecks','B')
c(64,'Adams conjecture and the explicitly incomplete étale proof','Q',[57,58,59,60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68],'1660-2090','potential proof; incomplete','The complex Adams assertion for a finite CW complex says that for an integer k, J(ψ^k x−x) is killed by a power of k. The attached potential proof reduces this to prime k and assumes a completed étale sphere-fibration construction and comparison.','Explicitly incomplete; local conjectures and rigidification gaps are stated.','Prime reduction uses ψ^(ab)−1=ψ^a(ψ^b−1)+(ψ^a−1), compatibility of J, and finite generation/torsion arguments in the specified setting. None of the assumed sphere-fibration properties is inferred from the diagram alone.','The published sketch is Quillen,Topology7(1968)111–116, received25Nov1967. Friedlander1973§6,Theorem6.8 completes the complex étale route. Quillen’s Topology10(1971)67–80 proves the Adams conjecture by a different, non-étale method; received3July1970.','forms_QuillenEtale;forms_Friedlander;forms_QuillenAdams','B',problem='Supply the missing completed étale sphere-fibration/comparison theorem without conflating the later independent proof.')
c(65,'Frobenius, Adams operations and the Grassmannian reduction','Q',[57,58,59,60],'1670-1804','Frobenius; degré p^n','In characteristic p, Frobenius pullback of a vector bundle has K-class ψ^p[E]. On a rank-n vector space its map on the punctured fiber has degree p^n under prime-to-p comparison. Universal quotient bundles on Grassmannians reduce the complex-bundle problem to smooth projective simply connected models.','Reduction sketched; rank and Grassmannian conventions alternate.','After the splitting principle, F^*L=L^p and ψ^p has that value on line bundles. The Frobenius map has algebraic degree p^n; after removing the origin it gives the corresponding sphere degree. Grassmannian of N-planes in n+N-space has a canonical quotient bundle of rank n, so these two labels are compatible and are not a transcription error by themselves.','Quillen1968 equations(3)–(4) and the subsequent Grassmannian reduction state this exact mechanism. Friedlander1973 retains the prime-to-residue-characteristic completion restriction.','forms_QuillenEtale;forms_Friedlander;forms_LocalChecks','B')
c(66,'Prime-to-p comparison and completion detects p-primary order','Q',[59,60,61],'1740-1841','r power of p','A smooth proper model over the strict localization at p gives the prime-to-p étale comparison required in the sketch; geometric simply connectedness and an ample line bundle are among its hypotheses. A finite cyclic obstruction whose completion away from p vanishes has p-power order.','Comparison used; obstruction argument given assuming the pro-sphere theory.','For Z/rZ, the ℓ-primary completion is zero for all ℓ≠p iff r has no prime divisors other than p. The r=0 infinite-cyclic case cannot be included: its ℓ-adic completion is nonzero. The source’s comparison is not extended to arbitrary nonproper or wildly ramified families.','Quillen1968 pp112–113 states the comparison input; Friedlander1973 Proposition6.6 gives the needed completed-fibration kernel statement for finite complexes.','forms_QuillenEtale;forms_Friedlander;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Keep the finite obstruction and prime-to-p hypotheses explicit in the completion step.')
c(67,'Pro-sphere representability and vector-bundle complement fibrations','Q',[61,62,63,64],'1813-1951','conjecture; rigidify','The sketch asks whether the stable functor of oriented pro-sphere fibrations is represented by a completed BSF, and separately needs punctured vector bundles to define functorial completed sphere fibrations compatible with complex realization. These are different requirements.','Representability is conjectural; the author says the stronger conjecture is not needed. Rigidification/functoriality of the bundle construction is the stated gap.','A locally free rank-r bundle with r≥2 over a connected noetherian pointed scheme has a completed punctured fiber equivalent to S^(2r−1) under Friedlander’s prime-to-all-residue-characteristics and profinite-π1 hypotheses. His theorem identifies the homotopy fiber; its extra pro-L condition is needed for the displayed long exact sequence.','Friedlander1973 Theorem5.3(pp37–38) supplies the precise vector-bundle fibration result, and§6 uses it to complete the complex Adams argument. It does not by itself establish every broad proSF representability formulation in the manuscript.','forms_QuillenEtale;forms_Friedlander','C',problem='Separate the representability conjecture from the weaker fibration theorem actually used.')
c(68,'Conditional additivity of the étale J construction','Q',[64,65,66,67],'1924-2060','J(E+F)=J(E)+J(F)','Assuming the completed punctured-bundle fibration construction and its base-change/realization compatibility, it defines an additive J on K0. Exact-sequence extensions are compared on a flag-bundle or universal parameter family; projective bundles and ample twists permit reduction to globally generated bundles.','Proof sketched conditional on the earlier conjectural construction.','The flag/projective-bundle splitting map is sufficiently injective for the chosen cohomological comparison. A universal extension family connects a short exact sequence to its split form. The conclusion is not unconditional until the fibration and rigidification assumptions are supplied.','Quillen1968 Proposition11 and pp114–116 carry the conditional argument; Friedlander1973§6 supplies its later foundation for the stated complex Adams application.','forms_QuillenEtale;forms_Friedlander','B',problem='Locate the exact functorial rigidification used to make the conditional J construction additive.')
c(69,'Every K0 class as a bundle minus a trivial bundle','Q',[67,68],'2047-2090','E−n','On a noetherian scheme with an ample line bundle, every class of K0(vector bundles) is of the form[E]−n for a vector bundle E and an integer n≥0.','Final lemma with explicit identities.','An ample twist makes bundles globally generated. A surjection from a finite free bundle onto a vector bundle splits locally, so its kernel is a vector bundle and −[F]=[K]−N. For a difference[A]−[B], take a finite free surjection onto a suitable globally generated construction of B, with the manuscript’s line-bundle identities handling the twist. Exact polynomial identities are checked separately; affine global generation must not be assumed for every bundle.','Quillen1968 Lemma14 gives precisely the noetherian ample-line-bundle statement and proof. The source is published and the hypothesis is stronger than merely quasi-compact.','forms_QuillenEtale;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Check all twist/dual signs in the final K0 identities.')

# Handwritten formal-category/de Rham notes, dated6September1968, not the typed10September witness.
c(80,'Formal categories and the inverse question','C',[22],'36-58','groupoïde formel ?','A formal category over X0 is encoded by a complete augmented algebra with source/target maps and a coassociative, counital completed comultiplication. The question asks whether a formal neighborhood of the identity section automatically has an inversion.','Definition and explicit open question.','For a one-object complete commutative formal bialgebra over a field, the convolution-geometric-series construction gives an antipode: the nth term lands in the nth augmentation power, so it converges. Extending that proof to a source/target Hopf algebroid requires the balanced tensor products, completeness and composability hypotheses; a bialgebra proof alone does not settle every pro-algebra formulation.','Formal Lie/groupoid theory supplies inverses in the standard infinitesimal groupoid/formally smooth integration setting. No exact unrestricted theorem matching all of the manuscript’s admissible-pro-algebra hypotheses was located. The one-object answer is established; the full statement is retained qualified.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_HopfAlgebroid;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Does the precise admissible formal-category definition force an antipode without extra smoothness?')
c(81,'Infinitesimal differential and differential operators','C',[22,23],'49-69','pr2−pr1 mod I²','The difference of target and source functions modulo I² defines a derivation d:O→I/I². A linear functional on the nth infinitesimal jet algebra gives a differential operator of order≤n; dualizing composition gives a filtered algebra when the continuous dual and completed tensor product are compatible.','Construction and “suite à développer.”','The product difference t(fg)−s(fg) reduces modulo I² to f·dg+g·df. Iterated commutators with functions correspond to multiplication by n+1 augmentation differences, which vanishes in the nth quotient. Finite local projectivity of jet terms makes the tensor-dual passage transparent.','Established jet/principal-parts and Lie–Rinehart enveloping-algebra construction. The source’s pro-limit/ind-limit and duality conventions are not certified without their finiteness hypotheses.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_StacksDiff;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Develop the dual algebra with exact topology, limit and finite-projectivity hypotheses.')
c(82,'Extending a degree-one derivation and the square-zero gap','C',[23,24],'69-98','δ1δ0=0','A degree1 derivation of ΛΩ is determined by its values on O and Ω satisfying the scalar Leibniz identity. To make it a differential, its square must vanish on both degree0 and degree1 generators; vanishing only on O is insufficient.','Extension asserted; the source claims the degree0 condition implies the full square-zero property.','Over Q let Ω have basis e1,e2,e3 and d0=0, de1=e1∧e2, de2=e2∧e3, de3=0. Then d1d0=0 but d²e1=−e1∧e2∧e3≠0. The degree error in the scalar Leibniz formula is separately recorded.','The literal implication is false. The corrected square-zero condition is the standard Chevalley–Eilenberg/Lie–Rinehart condition. Exact exterior-algebra computation is saved.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Add the missing degree1 square-zero condition and check whether it was lost in transcription.')
c(83,'Anchor, bracket, Leibniz and Jacobi','C',[24,25,26],'99-197','Pourquoi zéro ??','For Ω finite locally free, a square-zero exterior derivation defines an anchor on T=Ω∨ and a bracket by dω(X,Y)=a(X)ω(Y)−a(Y)ω(X)−ω([X,Y]). It satisfies anchor compatibility, the scalar Leibniz rule and Jacobi exactly when the corresponding degree0/degree1 square-zero identities hold.','Anchor and bracket calculations given; Jacobi explicitly questioned.','The scalar rule follows by expanding d(fω). The anchor commutator follows from d²f=0. Evaluating d²ω on X,Y,Z gives the Jacobiator paired with ω once the anchor identity holds. The preceding three-dimensional example yields a nonzero Jacobiator, so the written degree0 hypothesis alone cannot prove the questioned zero.','Established Lie–Rinehart/CE equivalence under finite local freeness and full d²=0. The question is explained by a concrete missing hypothesis rather than by a sign repair alone.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Resolve the Jacobi step by checking the actual hypotheses on δ².')
c(84,'Chevalley–Eilenberg complexes and exterior duality','C',[26,27],'199-214','correspondent exactement','For a finite locally free Lie–Rinehart module T, the CE algebra Hom(ΛT,O) identifies with Λ(T∨), and its differential is the exterior derivation determined by the anchor and bracket. This gives inverse constructions between Lie–Rinehart structures and square-zero de Rham-type exterior algebras.','Referred to earlier Bourbaki notes, with finite-local-freeness inserted.','The alternating multilinear CE formula is evaluated in degrees0,1 and then extended by graded Leibniz. The natural Λ(T∨)→Hom(ΛT,O) is an isomorphism for finite locally free T, but not for arbitrary flat modules.','Classical Lie–Rinehart and Chevalley–Eilenberg theory. The inserted finite-local-freeness is essential to the claimed dual equivalence and is preserved.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_LocalChecks','E')
c(85,'Quillen formal-groupoid equivalence and the flatness boundary','C',[27],'215-218','Th. de Quillen','The manuscript attributes to Quillen a characteristic-zero equivalence between I-adic formal categories with flat conormal Ω and exterior de Rham algebras with flat Ω, including SymΩ≅grA. The exact flat-only quantifier is stronger than the familiar finite-projective Lie–Rinehart PBW formulation.','Theorem attributed without proof; Cartier comparison stated.','For finite locally free Lie algebroids, filtered enveloping algebra/PBW and continuous jet duality produce the familiar infinitesimal groupoid correspondence. With merely flat Ω, dualization, completion and PBW cannot be replaced by finite-projective arguments without proof. No counterexample to the exact flat-only theorem is asserted.','Rinehart1963 supplies projective PBW foundations; modern Hopf/Lie-algebroid work treats differentiation/integration with explicit hypotheses, and derived formal-moduli equivalences concern a different category. The precise1968 flat-only theorem and its first circulation were not located in the searched primary sources.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_HopfAlgebroid;forms_FormalModuli','C',problem='Locate or prove the exact characteristic-zero flat-conormal theorem attributed to Quillen.',confidence='medium')
c(86,'Divided-power variant and comultiplication','C',[27,31],'220-221,343','relations ... Δ','A divided-power formal-groupoid version must specify compatibility of the comultiplication with the divided-power ideals and completed tensor-product PD structure. Merely putting unrelated PD operations on the augmentation ideals is insufficient to define a PD Hopf algebroid.','Explicit question on compatibility; final section is only a heading.','A PD morphism must satisfy Δ(γ_n(x))=γ_n(Δx) in the target PD ideal. This is additional structure, not a formal consequence of an arbitrary ring comultiplication and arbitrary PD choices. In characteristic0 the canonical divided powers on a Q-algebra remove this particular freedom; in positive characteristic they do not.','Crystalline/PD-envelope theory provides the correct compatibility framework. No complete theorem can be recovered from the final bare heading; its missing hypotheses are not supplied as manuscript content.','forms_StacksCrystals;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Formulate the PD compatibility and the missing positive-characteristic analogue.')
c(87,'Stratifications give integrable connections','C',[28,29],'225-282','descente; connexion','A descent/stratification isomorphism over the infinitesimal groupoid reduces at first order to a connection. Its cocycle condition at second order implies curvature zero. Conversely, in the usual characteristic-zero smooth or finite-projective formal integration setting, an integrable connection gives a Taylor stratification.','Forward construction derived; converse reserved for the attributed Quillen theorem.','Expand the first-order descent map as1+∇. The cocycle coefficient of two infinitesimal directions is the commutator of covariant derivatives minus the bracket term. Thus flatness is forced. The Taylor reconstruction uses division by factorials and the specified completion; it is not a positive-characteristic argument.','Established infinitesimal/crystalline stratification theory. The standard characteristic-zero theorem does not verify the manuscript’s unrestricted flat-conormal extension without the latter’s integration theorem.','forms_StacksCrystals;forms_LieRinehart;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Identify the exact Taylor/stratification theorem under the manuscript’s flat-only hypotheses.')
c(88,'Connections, curvature and signs','C',[29,30,31],'258-331','δ_M²; K(X,Y)','Given a square-zero de Rham algebra, a connection extends by ∇(ω⊗m)=dω⊗m+(−1)^degω ω∧∇m. Its square is Ω-linear curvature. Under finite local freeness, vanishing is equivalent to [∇X,∇Y]−∇[X,Y]=0. The manuscript’s K uses the opposite overall sign.','Computations and equivalence asserted; several indices/signs damaged.','The graded sign cancels the dω terms in ∇², making curvature O-linear. A degree0 curvature check suffices because the base differential already squares to zero. Expanding the bracket expression shows the overall sign relative to the manuscript definition; its zero locus is unchanged. The repeated X,X pair cannot represent an alternating two-variable curvature.','Established connection/CE identities. The missing graded sign and duplicated argument are recorded as readings, while the deliberate opposite curvature convention is not labelled false.','forms_LieRinehart;forms_StacksCrystals;forms_LocalChecks','E',problem='Recover the sign and two-variable argument without changing the curvature convention.')
c(89,'The missing integrability in the final equivalence','C',[31],'333-335','Ω-connexions','Descent data for the formal infinitesimal groupoid correspond to integrable connections in the standard characteristic-zero setting, not to arbitrary connections.','Final statement omits “integrable” despite the preceding curvature discussion.','On A²_Q, the rank1 connection∇=d+xdy has curvature dx∧dy≠0. It is a valid connection but cannot satisfy the second-order cocycle condition of a stratification. All the stated characteristic-zero, smoothness and finite-free conormal hypotheses hold.','The literal all-connections assertion is false. The standard integrable-connection theorem is the precise corrected formulation; the original ink must decide whether the adjective was omitted or struck.','forms_StacksCrystals;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Check the missing integrability qualifier in§11.')
c(90,'De Rham versus formal-groupoid cohomology and crystals','C',[31],'335-343','topos convenable ?','Under the appropriate characteristic-zero infinitesimal-groupoid and integrable-module hypotheses, the de Rham/CE complex compares with the normalized groupoid Čech–Alexander complex. The author asks whether this generalization can be reduced to his crystalline theorem inside a suitable topos.','Comparison asserted; reduction to crystals asked; the PD variant is undeveloped.','Antisymmetrization and the formal Poincaré lemma give the usual local comparison in a smooth/finite-projective setting, and descent globalizes it. A quasi-isomorphism for arbitrary flat Ω is not supplied by invoking an unrelated derived formal-moduli equivalence.','Established standard comparison in the smooth infinitesimal/crystalline setting. The exact flat-conormal extension and its proposed topos reduction remain unlocated as stated; this is a bounded search result, not a claim of present-day open status.','forms_StacksCrystals;forms_LieRinehart;forms_FormalModuli','C',problem='Find a precise crystalline topos realizing the stated general formal-groupoid comparison.',confidence='medium')

