c(95,'Typed witnesses, dates and attribution','Q',[2,32,42,50,57],'1-45,740-746,1125-1132,1369-1393,1656-1670','10 septembre1968','The two typed Tapis copies carry10September1968 and have differing margins. The attached positivity offprint and potential Adams proof are by Daniel Quillen. A blankscan42 is not missing mathematical text. The undated attached preprint is not dated from the typed notes.','Document metadata.','Compare both supplied date lines and their author/title pages. The positivity paper records received27February1968; the published Adams sketch records received25November1967. These are separate evidence from manuscript date and publication year.','Bibliographic facts verified against the supplied witnesses and opened publication sources; no earliest-circulation date is inferred from a received date.','forms_QuillenPositive;forms_QuillenEtale','F')
c(96,'Handwritten date and later bibliography sheet','C',[20,22],'1-36','6 septembre1968','The handwritten formal-category notes are dated6September1968. The preceding Quillen bibliography lists later publications including1970 and “to appear,” so that sheet cannot be used to date all adjacent notes to1968.','Title/date and bibliography.','Read the explicit date and eleven bibliography entries separately. The cover supplied in K162-5-02 labels Alexandre’s notes1968 and supplies no mathematical theorem.','Document-level evidence only; order of compilation is not established by the supplied transcription.','forms_ArchiveMetadata','F')
c(96,'Cover sheet','D',[2],'1-11','Notes d’Alexandre1968','The separate cover identifies the dossier as notes of Alexandre,1968; it adds no claim and no independent date for the later attached publications.','Cover only.','Directly read all11lines; no mathematics omitted.','Metadata retained as a source rather than counted as an unresolved theorem.','forms_ArchiveMetadata','F')
c(97,'Blass translation outline and missing final sections','B',[1,2],'1-86','Piotr Blass; §16','The supplied text is a translation attributed to Piotr Blass, with an outline of16sections. The translator says§16 is not written and requests discussion with Grothendieck. The transcription wrapper’s1987 label is not an independently read date on the cover.','Cover, translator note and plan.','Distinguish the wrapper metadata from the cover’s actual wording. Only the surviving text is mapped; headings for unwritten later sections do not supply theorems.','Archival metadata; no unique theorem to classify. The later SGA7 publication explicitly credits the forthcoming EGA V pencil treatment but does not date this typed translation.','forms_ArchiveMetadata;forms_SGA7Katz','F')
c(98,'Survivre sheets quarantined from mathematical chronology','B',[75,76],'2786-2867','Survivre','The final sheets list Survivre publications and distribution details; they are distinct attached material and cannot date every preceding mathematical draft. They contain no additional EGA theorem.','Nonmathematical attached lists.','Read both sheets through the end; no mathematical proposition or project crosslink was extracted from their titles.','Retained in coverage and chronology metadata only. No external private-context project lead is inferred.','forms_ArchiveMetadata','F')

c(43,'Fixed coefficients do not detect integral homotopy','Q',[4,34],'138-145,826-833','n’importe quel anneau non nul','The converse claiming that equivalence of bounded locally constant derived categories over every fixed nonzero coefficient ring detects weak homotopy equivalence is false. Over Q it detects only rational cohomological information once the local systems are fixed.','The converse is expressly asserted for an arbitrary nonzero ring.','Let X=M(Z/p,n), n≥2, a finite simply connected Moore complex. Its reduced integral homology is Z/p in degree n, so it is not weakly contractible. Over Q its two-cell differential is multiplication by p, hence invertible. All Q-local systems are constant and their positive Ext groups are the rational cohomology of X with Hom coefficients, which vanishes. Bounded Postnikov induction therefore gives D^b_lc(X;Q)≃D^b(Q). Taking the face category of a finite triangulation gives the category-nerve formulation.','Negative for the stated fixed arbitrary nonzero coefficient ring. Hatcher Example2.40 supplies the precise Moore-space construction; the derived-category deduction and rational cellular computation are independently checked here. The all-integral/all-coefficient formulation is a separate stronger criterion.','forms_Hatcher;forms_Derived;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Specify which coefficient rings detect the intended integral homotopy type.')

c(32,'Grassmann chart and its choice of splitting','B',[72],'2692-2712','canonically isomorphic','For an exact sequence 0→E′→E→E″→0 with E′ finite locally free of rank n, the chart of rank-n quotients restricting to an isomorphism on E′ is the sheaf of retractions E→E′. It is a torsor under Hom(E″,E′), and is identified with the vector bundle only after choosing a splitting. The basis decompositions immediately preceding the sentence do supply such a splitting.','The chart proof says “canonically” after discussing chosen basis decompositions; the scope of “such an exact sequence” is ambiguous.','A quotient in the chart can be identified with E′ and hence is a retraction. Differences of retractions factor uniquely through E″. Translating a chosen splitting by u∈Hom(E″,E′) translates the coordinate origin, so no choice-free zero section exists in the general exact-sequence formulation.','The ordinary affine chart construction is established. The chosen-basis statement is valid; an extension to arbitrary exact sequences as a canonical vector-bundle identification requires the missing choice. This is the same qualification recorded in cote25, but the typed contexts are retained separately.','forms_StacksGrass;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Determine whether “canonical” remains relative to the preceding chosen basis splitting.')

c(33,'Arbitrary-morphism irreducibility bound','B',[57],'2153-2163','without the hypothesis of normality and of non-ramification','The recoverable proposal in8.17(a) is that for geometrically irreducible X and arbitrary f with dim f(X)≥3, the locus of hyperplanes with geometrically reducible pullback has codimension≥2; a following sentence proposes at least that it contain no whole hyperplane in the dual parameter space.','The wider proposal is compressed into a sentence fragment in the translation; its named assumptions and proposed bound are visible.','Let X=Bl_p(P³) and f be its blowdown. X is smooth, proper and geometrically integral, and dim f(X)=3. Every hyperplane through p pulls back to the union of its strict transform and the exceptional divisor. These hyperplanes form a hyperplane in the dual P³, so both proposed conclusions fail. Fibers are not equidimensional.','Poonen–Slavov Example1.7 gives this exact obstruction. Theorem1.5 gives the bound when all nonempty fibers have one dimension; Theorem1.8 isolates divisorial exceptions from fiber-dimension jumps for arbitrary morphisms. The authors’ first arXiv version is23January2020, the checked revision4August2020; final issue dating is kept separate.','forms_PoonenSlavov;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Separate the false arbitrary-morphism bound in8.17(a) from the equidimensional-fiber and divisorial-exception theorems.')
c(34,'Surface sections with at most two components','B',[57],'2162-2179','at most two irreducible','For a smooth proper geometrically integral surface immersed in projective space, under the separable nondefective conormal alternative used in8.8 (or the smooth dual-defective case), sufficiently general sections outside a codimension≥2 exceptional locus are connected and have at most one ordinary node, hence at most two geometric irreducible components. A smooth quadric surface has a divisorial family of reducible tangent sections.','Remark8.17(b) sketches the consequence but has several illegible words in its dual-map qualification.','The tangent section of x0x3−x1x2=0 at[1:0:0:0] is x1x2=0, two lines. For the two-component bound, normalization of a connected reduced curve smooth except at one node can have at most two connected components: the node identifies at most two branches, so it can connect at most two components.','The quadric calculation and two-branch argument are checked. SGA7XVII3.3–3.5 supplies the immersed, non-supersingular conormal mechanism; the unreadable qualification is not restored verbatim. This does not assert the full subsequent finite-exceptional-set iff.','forms_SGA7Katz;forms_Benoist2011;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Recover the dual-map qualification and the exact at-most-two-components clause in8.17(b).')
c(35,'Finite-exceptional-set irreducibility criterion','B',[58,59],'2193-2258','it is necessary and sufficient','For proper integral X of dimension d≥2 over an algebraically closed field, with finite closed T, smooth X−T and unramified f there,8.18 proposes a codimension≥2 bad-section criterion using: an irreducible(d−1)-dimensional section through each x∈T; and either dual defect on X−T or an irreducible singular section associated with the dual divisor. The transcription’s singular/nonsingular adjective is undecided, and it writes dimension1 where d−1 is needed for general d.','Proof sketched for normal X using openness and the finitely many candidate bad divisors, then normalization is invoked without details.','For normal X, let U be the open locus of integral sections of the right dimension. Outside a codimension≥2 set, bad parameters lie in the hyperplanes H_x for x∈T and the conormal image divisor when nondefective. Every such irreducible divisor has generic point in U iff it meets U, explaining the indicated witness tests. The nonnormal normalization step and unreadable adjective need their exact hypotheses checked rather than assumed.','The open-locus and divisor-testing mechanism is established; EGAIV3 and SGA7 provide the checked inputs. No exact published theorem matching every damaged iff clause and normalization step was located. The source supports a precise residual formulation task, not an assertion of a new open theorem.','forms_EGAIV3;forms_SGA7Katz;forms_Benoist2011;forms_LocalChecks','C',problem='Recover the finite-exceptional-set iff, especially the singular-section adjective and the reduction through normalization.')

c(40,'The presheaf-topos construction is not full','Q',[2,32],'60-70,761-771','n’a plus rien de pleinement fidèle, semble-t-il ??','Sending a small category C to its presheaf topos does not make all geometric morphisms arise from functors between the indexing categories. The source explicitly doubts full faithfulness; the distinction from the fully faithful nerve remains essential.','Explicit parenthetical question.','Let D be the ordered set of all integers. The filtered-colimit functor Psh(D)→Set over D^op preserves all colimits and finite limits and has the constant-presheaf functor as right adjoint. It defines a point of Psh(D). It is not evaluation at any n: it sends every representable y(m) to a singleton, whereas evaluation at n sends y(n−1) to the empty set. A functor from the terminal category to D chooses n and induces evaluation, so this point proves non-fullness. This does not assert loss of every form of faithfulness.','Established presheaf-topos point theory; SGA4IV Exercise7.6 distinguishes the representable/essential points, and the explicit nonrepresentable filtered-colimit point is independently proved here. Seminar and publication dates are kept separate.','forms_SGA4I;forms_LocalChecks','B',problem='Is the presheaf-topos construction fully faithful, as the nerve is?')

