byid['C-SA23-forms-004']['refs'].append('forms_StacksFieldDim')
byid['C-SA23-forms-004']['computation']+=' The explicit reference request in2.6 is answered by Stacks10.116.5: finite Noether normalization remains finite and injective after a field extension, proving dimension invariance.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-012']['computation']='Every irreducible component of the proper incidence dominates the normal dual projective space. In its Stein factorization the finite generic fiber is geometrically connected, so its reduced algebra is one purely inseparable field extension; its degree need not be one. Every component of the finite Stein factor dominates the base, hence its reduction is integral. Over a normal affine base A, each element b of the finite reduced algebra has a p-power in Frac(A), and that power is integral, hence lies in A. Thus the reduced Stein factor is finite radicial and surjective, a universal homeomorphism. Nilpotents do not alter connectedness, and the Stein fibers are geometrically connected, so all original fibers are geometrically connected. In characteristic zero the purely inseparable extension is trivial. This is a present-run proof, retaining the source’s properness and component hypotheses.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-020']['fate']='Katz’s SGA7 II, XVII1.1–1.2 and Proposition3.3 distinguish ordinary from ordinary non-supersingular quadratic points. In cote25 scans139–141, physical5520–5620, the early definitions have live/cancelled alternatives; the explicit correction to a smooth projective tangent quadric is in scans158–160, physical6261–6374. These are distinct witnesses, checked at both endpoints by root, and neither erases the Blass wording.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-039']['computation']='For the double category of commutative squares in an ordinary category C, the twofold nerve is Fun([m]×[n],C); transposition interchanges directions. For an arbitrary strict double category, the iterated nerve has the Segal isomorphisms in each direction, and conversely these recover composition, identities and interchange. Globularity is an additional discreteness constraint, not a consequence of Segal conditions alone.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-025']['open_flag']='State the fixed-base divisor and geometrically irreducible generic-fiber hypotheses in the projection version.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-025']['computation']+=' The image function field is k(a_1,…,a_(p−1)), not K(a_1,…)=K; projection from a q-plane has fibers of dimension q+1, so image dimension is at least dim f(X)−q−1. The equality2 in the displayed geometric-irreducibility criterion must be a bound≥2.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-043']['class']='E'
byid['C-SA23-forms-043']['computation']='Stasheff1963 Theorem17, printed pp282–283, constructs A_(p−1) spaces admitting no A_p structure. Its Moore example for p=5 and n=3 has reduced homology Z_(5) in degree5, is A4 and hence homotopy associative, but has no A5 structure and cannot have loop-space homotopy type. The full theorem and obstruction/Steenrod argument were read. Connectedness supplies a homotopy inverse, so the example also meets the group-object condition in the ordinary homotopy category. May’s Theorem13.4 supplies the positive recognition theorem with the full A-infinity structure.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-043']['fate']='The negative answer for bare homotopy-group objects was known before these September1968 notes: Stasheff, Homotopy Associativity of H-Spaces I, Theorem17 (August1963), pp282–283, explicitly credits the examples to Adams. Received8March1962; the two1959 presentation dates do not independently date first circulation of this exact example. May1972 Theorem13.4 is the later checked positive A-infinity recognition formulation. The1970 book Example7.6 was not accessible and is not used as proof.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-081']['statement']='For a locally split exact sequence 0→E′→E→E″→0 with E′ finite locally free of rank n, the chart of rank-n quotients restricting to an isomorphism on E′ is the sheaf of retractions E→E′. It is a torsor under Hom(E″,E′), identified with that additive sheaf only after a chosen splitting; when E″ is also finite locally free this is a vector bundle. The basis decompositions immediately preceding the sentence supply the splitting and finite-rank hypotheses. For an arbitrary exact sequence the retraction functor need not be locally nonempty.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-081']['computation']='A quotient in the chart can be identified with E′ and hence is a retraction. Differences of retractions factor uniquely through E″. Translating a chosen splitting by u in Hom(E″,E′) changes the origin, so there is no choice-free zero section. Local existence requires local splitting: 0→2Z→Z→Z/2→0 has no retraction near the prime2, even after a faithfully flat base change. The manuscript’s basis decompositions meet these requirements; the generalized exact-sequence wording needs them stated.'
byid['C-SA23-forms-051']['status_on_pages']='A noetherian question is crossed out and replaced by the coherence assertion; the expected abelian envelope remains dependent on the meaning of generated.'
